# Briefing — orienting a newcomer on a subject

`context/instructions/briefing.md` is the contract for a **briefing**: a document
that orients a newcomer — a new agent session or a new person — on a **subject**
(a mission, a subsystem, a workflow). Read it with
`context/instructions/ingestion.md` (how sources are converted and cited) and
`context/instructions/wiki.md` (the citation and evidence conventions).

## When this applies (no-op rule)

- Use it to brief someone on a subject that already exists. If the subject is a
  single decision or a single component, a `decisions/` or `architecture/` page is
  the right home — do not write a briefing. Say which case applies.

## The rule: derive, don't trust

A briefing **composes cited artifacts**; it never generates summary prose it
cannot cite. Every **volatile** fact — a version, a path, a count, a state — is
paired with the **command that derives it**, so the reader re-derives it instead
of trusting a copy that will go stale. A fact with no deriving command is flagged;
a stated value that could drift is a defect.

## Frontmatter

| Field | Required | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| `kind` | yes | `briefing` |
| `subject` | yes | what it briefs on (a slug or a path) |
| `status` | yes | `draft` · `active` · `archived` |
| `summary` | yes | one or two lines |
| `sources` | yes | the artifacts it composes (cited) |
| `verified` | no | date + by whom the delta was second-verified |

## Section contract

- `## Artifacts` — the concrete pieces, each cited (file, command, decision).
- `## Map` — how they fit together and the order they are read.
- `## Roles` — who owns what; where the reader stands.
- `## Rules` — the binding constraints and gates.
- `## Derived facts` — each volatile fact paired with the command that derives it.
- `## Delta` — a dated record of what changed since the last briefing,
  second-verified.

## Boundary

- Facts and their citations follow `context/instructions/wiki.md` and
  `context/instructions/ingestion.md`; conversion follows `ingestion.md`.
- A briefing orients; it does not decide (a `decision`), specify (a `plan`) or
  document a component (an `architecture` page).
- Keep the six sections in the order above; add none.
